    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "When you were in school—elementary, middle, or high school—did your teachers ever comment that you had trouble paying attention, or that you were fidgety or talked too much in class?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, definitely. Yeah, my mom kept a whole file of those notes from my elementary school teachers. I was always getting in trouble because I'd just pop right out of my seat during quiet time, or I'd be talking over the other kids. And I was terrible about blurting out answers before the teacher even finished asking the question. I just felt like I already knew where they were going with it, so why wait, right? \n\nIt actually reminds me of this one time in fourth grade, Mrs. Gable's class. She had this huge terrarium in the back of the room with a bearded dragon named Spike. We were supposed to be doing a math worksheet, but I was so fixated on watching Spike eat these crickets. Did you know they can eat, like, dozens of crickets in a week? My nephew just got a reptile, actually, a leopard gecko, and my sister is absolutely terrified of the live food. She makes her husband use these long tweezers to drop the mealworms in. I told her she should have just gotten him a hamster, but apparently hamsters are nocturnal and keep you up all night running on that squeaky little wheel. I had a hamster named Barnaby when I was eight, and my dad ended up having to oil the wheel with WD-40 at two in the morning because nobody could sleep... \n\nBut anyway, yeah, the teachers definitely noticed. Lots of notes home about the talking and not staying in my seat.",
